{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794665010,"nonce":"0RV2Kw4t+7b8Nh6YOg6qIqsoraI=","title":"a library's Haxe classes are written once for each runtime","message":"hxn-gen writes a library's Haxe classes three times, under binding/hl, binding/cpp and binding/cppia, and a package ships all three. For hxn-window, 55 of 62 files are byte-identical in all three (every record, enum and wire serializer); the 7 interface classes have the same public API in each and differ only in how they reach native code: HashLink's private `@:hlNative` class over `hl.Abstract` handles and `hl.Bytes` conversions, hxcpp's calls through the extern class over the C++ glue with a `cpp.RawPointer` handle and a GC finalizer, and CPPIA's extern declarations of the hxcpp class. `hxn.Bind` only picks one directory. The user, 2026-11-14: hxn exists so these are not duplicated; no file per runtime; if hxn.Bind cannot do it, a macro does. hxn's haxelib ships the macro as part of the core library, and libraries such as hxn-window reuse it rather than redeclaring or regenerating it (6a7d383 made hxn's classes the `hxn` haxelib packages depend on).\n\nThe original scope had `hxn.Bind` build the classes from the manifest; on 2026-11-11 it was built as a resolver over pre-generated files, to keep one emitter. That choice is what produced the copies.\n\nPlan:\n- hxn-gen writes one set of classes: records, enums and serializers as now, and each interface class as its public API only, signatures and docs, under a build macro from the hxn haxelib.\n- The macro reads the library's manifest, which already records each parameter's and result's type in hxn's notation (`str`, `bytes_mut`, `Pot?`, `future\u003clist\u003cPoint\u003e\u003e`, `fn(Counter, str) -\u003e i32`), whether a call fails or blocks, and whether an interface is shared or weighed, and adds for the runtime being compiled the handle field, the natives, the conversions in each body, and the finalizer or release.\n- The Rust adapters, the C header and the C++ glue stay generated in Rust from the same model; the manifest is the contract between them and the macro.\n- A package and hxn-window ship one class path, their manifest and their native libraries; `hxn.Bind` stops choosing among directories.\n\nTo work out: whether a CPPIA script can compile against the class as the host has it (with `-D dll_import`), so it needs no declarations; how results over time (`Hxn\u003cLibrary\u003eFuture` and the like), lent functions, cells, weak references and typed exceptions are expressed, since those classes are generated per library today; HashLink and hxcpp for wasm.","files":null}]}